<section class="page-lead page-shell" aria-labelledby="engineering-title">
  <p class="eyebrow">ForgeWire Labs · Research &amp; Engineering</p>
  <h1 id="engineering-title">Questions, implementation, and <em>evidence have different jobs.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead-copy">This page separates questions the organization investigates from behavior visible in public software. An overview can establish a project's direction; it does not establish a released implementation. Each example below is bounded by its own source and version.</p>
</section>

<section class="engineering-principles page-shell" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
  <div class="section-heading section-heading--stacked">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research questions · ongoing work</p>
    <h2 id="questions-title">Open questions, not published findings</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">These questions describe current lines of investigation. They are not claims of completed research results or capabilities implemented across the portfolio.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="engineering-examples">
    <article class="engineering-example engineering-example--wide">
      <p class="example-label">Question · model-assisted work</p>
      <h3>How can a person keep useful context and decision authority when software coordinates models and tools?</h3>
      <p>The public ForgeWire overview describes an application and runtime direction around context, tools, selected workflows, and reviewable outcomes. ForgeWire remains private development; the overview is not an application release.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview">Read the public ForgeWire overview <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>
    <article class="engineering-example">
      <p class="example-label">Question · distributed execution</p>
      <h3>What should be explicit when work is placed across machines?</h3>
      <p>Fabric explores a separate authenticated, policy-gated execution control plane. The public overview describes limited, configuration-dependent integration; it does not document a general remote service or release.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview/blob/85d1905b0bd8db420882f00065daff34833dab27/README.md#3-fabric-dispatch-and-remote-execution">Read the public Fabric scope <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>
    <article class="engineering-example">
      <p class="example-label">Question · state and evidence</p>
      <h3>What should a repository retain so later work can be inspected?</h3>
      <p>RepoPact Core provides a public implementation example for repository-owned records, validation, and typed operations. Its evidence is specific to RepoPact and its released CLI package.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/repopact-core">Inspect RepoPact Core <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="evidence-band" aria-labelledby="evidence-heading">
  <div class="page-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Public engineering evidence</p>
    <h2 id="evidence-heading">Examples from their own sources</h2>
    <div class="evidence-cards">
      <article>
        <p class="example-label">RepoPact Core · released CLI 3.1.3</p>
        <h3>Rust defines the semantics; the CLI exposes supported operations.</h3>
        <p>Core reads repository records, validates them, and prepares typed operations through a canonical Rust engine. Its stable published package is version-scoped; the source tree can continue to evolve separately.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/repopact-core/blob/614689b127cf52f801877cc48440f7e45c3709eb/README.md">Read the Core design notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="example-label">Anvil · developer preview 0.1.0a0</p>
        <h3>Planning state has a deliberately narrow owner.</h3>
        <p>Anvil stores planning records in a single-writer JSON file and links typed references to external work. Its API and schema can change; its documented scope excludes execution ownership and audit trails.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/anvil/tree/89ac5df34090e9ffde6a0b790620e9096fb0f15d">Read Anvil's source documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="example-label">SkillForge Academy · Windows app 1.3.2</p>
        <h3>A published application and a beta platform have separate maturity.</h3>
        <p>The released Windows app supports certification study. Newer course-platform development and its import/export behavior remain in beta and are not folded into the app's release claim.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/skillforge-academy">Inspect the public project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="proving-ground page-shell" aria-labelledby="proving-title">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Validation repository · not a released product</p>
    <h2 id="proving-title"><a href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/RP-proving-grounds">RP Proving Grounds <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h2>
